{"id": "b21529cf-eaad-4258-add7-04e0353b35f3", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll check your CloudTrail setup to see whether S3 object-level deletes and Lambda invocations are covered. Those are data events, so they only show up if a trail has data event selectors enabled for them. I'm checking the trails now.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_LFQyXHaLg5n4HCfXFWvKvB", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "cloudtrail-coverage-check", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes", "verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Determine whether CloudTrail in this environment would record (a) S3 object deletions (DeleteObject / DeleteObjects) in our S3 buckets and (b) Lambda function invocations (Invoke).",
  "context": "User asks a configuration-coverage question, not an incident. Background: S3 object-level operations and Lambda Invoke are DATA events, which are NOT logged by default; they require a trail (or event data store) with data event selectors (basic or advanced) covering AWS::S3::Object and AWS::Lambda::Function. Bucket-level S3 operations and Lambda management calls (CreateFunction, UpdateFunctionCode, etc.) are management events. Event selector details (readOnly/writeOnly, resource ARN scoping, advanced field selectors) determine coverage. Also check that trails are actually logging (get_trail_status IsLogging), whether they are multi-region, organization trails, and whether CloudTrail Lake event data stores exist with data event coverage.",
  "resources": ["Agent space account 012345678901, region us-east-1; also check other associated accounts and regions where trails are visible", "All CloudTrail trails and event data stores", "S3 buckets and Lambda functions in the account (list them so you can say which are/are not covered)"],
  "timeframe": "Current configuration as of 2026-11-19T15:31:30Z",
  "criteria": "For each trail/event data store report: name, home region, multi-region?, org trail?, IsLogging, management event settings (read/write), data event selectors for S3 and Lambda (all resources vs specific ARNs, read/write type, include/exclude). Then conclude per resource type: are S3 object deletes recorded? Are Lambda invocations recorded? Which specific buckets/functions are not covered? Note any gaps (e.g. only specific buckets, WriteOnly vs ReadOnly, trail not logging). If CloudTrail control-plane calls through use_aws are blocked, use whatever other route is available (e.g. the CloudTrail tool, CloudFormation/Config, or documentation) and clearly state what you could not verify. Do not make any mutating calls."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Check CloudTrail trails for S3 delete and Lambda invoke coverage"}], "created_at": null}

